<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Gervasio Artigas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1764–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as José Artigas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Gervasio Artigas led the rising of the Banda Oriental against Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1811 and became leader of the Eastern Province. Demanding independence and a federal union, he broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and in 1814–1815 drew the provinces of the Littoral into the Federal League as its Protector. Portuguese invasion from 1816 and defeat at Tacuarembó in 1820 ended his power, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> honours him as national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas.jpg" alt="José Gervasio Artigas" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Manuel Blanes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Manuel_Blanes_-_Artigas_en_la_Ciudadela.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
